Gemma

I've not had the pleasure of computing distances from reticles and certainly not the wrinkle associated with calculations when the horizon is not visible.

Nevertheless, I had a look at the code you provided and noted a curiosity in the next to the last line of code you provided.  I've never had reason to use the function `sinpi()` so I needed to look up what it did.  I don't think it does what you want.  Instead you should be using `asin()` there I think.  You'll also note in Lerczak and Hobbs (1998) Eqn 5 of the appendix, that `asin()` of the quantity of interest is very nearly 1 and hence the `asin()` can be eliminated.

I also learned that a "mil" is equivalent to 0.001 of a radian.  You'll note that your code computes "mil.deg2rad" to be 0.00098, so your calculation was pretty close.

On 16/08/2017 11:57, Gemma Veneruso wrote:

